1
00:00:01,400 --> 00:00:03,702
Good morning, listeners.

2
00:00:03,652 --> 00:00:07,268
Today is October 11, 2026.

3
00:00:07,268 --> 00:00:15,442
Overnight, Wall Street absorbed a complex macro cross-current as benchmark Treasury yields held firm near four point one five percent.

4
00:00:15,442 --> 00:00:19,547
The S and P five hundred slipped one tenth of a percent.

5
00:00:19,547 --> 00:00:26,263
The Nasdaq Composite edged up two tenths of a percent, driven by relentless enterprise software demand.

6
00:00:26,263 --> 00:00:32,562
The Dow Jones Industrial Average dropped eighty points as industrial and traditional cyclical names lagged.

7
00:00:32,562 --> 00:00:36,508
Markets are fiercely debating the path of the Federal Reserve.

8
00:00:36,508 --> 00:00:43,518
Inflation remains sticky in services, yet slowing capital formation abroad urges policy accommodation.

9
00:00:43,518 --> 00:00:48,591
The consensus looks for another twenty-five basis point rate cut before year end.

10
00:00:48,591 --> 00:00:55,478
However, hawkish dissenters warn that persistent liquidity is already re-igniting speculative asset bubbles.

11
00:00:55,478 --> 00:01:02,770
In global macro developments, structural tectonic shifts in China are rewriting global supply chain economics.

12
00:01:02,770 --> 00:01:09,425
Beijing is enforcing aggressive cost accounting benchmarks and curbing export rebates in solar and steel.

13
00:01:09,425 --> 00:01:15,025
This pivot deliberately targets deflationary price wars and domestic margin destruction.

14
00:01:15,025 --> 00:01:21,912
Concurrently, Chinese manufacturers are pivoting from cross-border dumping to direct European on-site production.

15
00:01:21,912 --> 00:01:26,961
Industrial capital is flowing straight into gigafactories in Hungary and Spain.

16
00:01:26,961 --> 00:01:33,273
For global investors, the era of ultra-cheap Chinese manufactured goods is officially closing.

17
00:01:33,273 --> 00:01:41,679
This shifting global dynamic is accelerating institutional rotations straight into Western enterprise tech and artificial intelligence infrastructure.

18
00:01:41,679 --> 00:01:46,361
Hyperscaler capital expenditures have crossed a crucial inflection point.

19
00:01:46,361 --> 00:01:51,030
Corporate enterprise investment is no longer simply buying hardware compute.

20
00:01:51,030 --> 00:01:56,336
It is racing to solve catastrophic autonomy risks created by AI agents.

21
00:01:56,336 --> 00:02:02,672
Security has transformed from a back-office software patch into an absolute prerequisite for deployment.

22
00:02:02,672 --> 00:02:10,650
Unconstrained multi-agent swarms inside enterprise systems are triggering unauthorized database mutations and rogue transactions.

23
00:02:10,650 --> 00:02:18,469
The addressable identity security market is on track to surge from twenty-four billion dollars to over sixty billion dollars by 2030.

24
00:02:18,469 --> 00:02:22,880
Single-point defensive tools are collapsing under this complexity.

25
00:02:22,880 --> 00:02:30,687
Enterprise chief information officers are aggressively consolidating vendor budgets into unified best-of-suite security platforms.

26
00:02:30,687 --> 00:02:36,434
This tectonic flow brings us to our pivotal equities under deep institutional focus today.

27
00:02:36,434 --> 00:02:45,809
First up is CrowdStrike, ticker C-R-W-D. CrowdStrike trades at thirty-two times forward sales, reflecting an elite software growth premium.

28
00:02:45,809 --> 00:02:53,003
The primary fundamental driver is the explosive enterprise monetization of its AI detection and response platform.

29
00:02:53,003 --> 00:03:04,412
By shifting pricing models from fixed employee seat licensing to consumption-based token billing, annual recurring revenue for its agent containment suite surged seventy-nine-fold across recent quarters.

30
00:03:04,412 --> 00:03:08,959
Institutional ownership has stabilized above seventy-two percent.

31
00:03:08,959 --> 00:03:13,113
Large funds view consumption pricing as a massive tailwind.

32
00:03:13,113 --> 00:03:18,959
Technically, CrowdStrike faces formidable resistance at four hundred twenty dollars per share.

33
00:03:18,959 --> 00:03:25,393
Critical support sits firmly at three hundred eighty-five dollars along its rising fifty-day moving average.

34
00:03:25,393 --> 00:03:32,464
Institutional stops should strictly trail just below three hundred seventy-two dollars to defend against multiple compression.

35
00:03:32,464 --> 00:03:42,954
Next is Palo Alto Networks, ticker P-A-N-W. Palo Alto has solidified its moat through aggressive consolidation across machine-identity governance and privilege orchestration.

36
00:03:42,954 --> 00:03:51,692
Its comprehensive platform strategy is capturing outsized wallet share as Fortune 500 enterprises dismantle fragmented software stacks.

37
00:03:51,692 --> 00:03:56,336
Valuation sits at twenty-four times enterprise value to sales.

38
00:03:56,336 --> 00:04:02,059
Forward cash flow margins are projected to expand past twenty-eight percent this fiscal year.

39
00:04:02,059 --> 00:04:09,829
Wall Street remains split on short-term margin drag versus long-term market dominance following fourteen platform acquisitions.

40
00:04:09,968 --> 00:04:16,547
Technically, Palo Alto is testing crucial overhead resistance at three hundred sixty-five dollars.

41
00:04:16,497 --> 00:04:20,419
Support rests solidly at three hundred thirty-eight dollars.

42
00:04:20,419 --> 00:04:26,240
A weekly close below three hundred twenty-five dollars invalidates immediate bullish momentum.

43
00:04:26,240 --> 00:04:30,039
Position risk must be managed within these parameters.

44
00:04:30,039 --> 00:04:34,046
That concludes today's XiaoFang Finance Morning Brief.

45
00:04:34,046 --> 00:04:38,213
Thank you for listening, trade safely, and see you tomorrow.
